The master of the first system, cut by the combined X-codes of indices i and j, is contained in the combined X-code of k if and only if the master of the second system, cut by the combined Y-codes of i and j, is contained in the combined Y-code of k.

The emptiness transfer, applied to the sign list that marks i and j positively and k negatively, at one more than the maximum of the three indices, equates nonemptiness of the corresponding master slices; each listed index lies strictly below that bound. Extensionality and propositional simplification identify each slice with the set difference of the double intersection against the code of k. A difference is empty exactly when the inclusion holds, so negating both sides of the nonemptiness equivalence yields the two inclusions.
